  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Short and long – eight in a row",
  "description": "Six against six in a large area with no goals, about 40 × 40 metres, split into two halves by a line of cones across the middle. The team on the ball keeps it as long as it can. Eight passes in a row score a point. A pass straight back to the player who just passed to you does not count. If the other team wins the ball, it starts counting from zero.\n\nTo get both short and long passes into the game: a pass across the middle line to a team-mate in the other half counts as two. So the team needs players both close to the ball and far away from it.\n\nVariation: two touches at most, or one when it flows – but the player who intercepts may take as many touches as they like. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Vary the distance: someone close for the short pass, someone far away for the long one."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Stand so that the pass to you does not go through an opponent."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Look for the long pass before the ball reaches you."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Short passes firm and along the ground, long ones so that your team-mate can control them."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
  "phase": "main",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "focus": [
